  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">It is expected that workers&#x2019; organizations in Sri Lanka evolve into inclusive, representative, and influential actors within the labour governance system, effectively organizing and advocating for the informal workforce that nearly accounts for 60 per cent of all workers, while advancing the leadership and agency of women and youth. Informal workers, women, and young workers gain sustained voice and bargaining power in social dialogue and policy-making processes, resulting in labour policies that better address informality, gender inequality, and intergenerational exclusion. Institutionalized collaboration among trade unions, the Ministry of Labour, UN agencies, and development partners drive a shift toward more democratic, equitable, and rights-based worker representation, strengthening social cohesion and inclusive labour governance in Sri Lanka.</narrative>
  </description>
